<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aizpute</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hasenpoth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aizpute, in German Hasenpoth, is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Kurzeme</a> region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it held the cathedral of the Bishopric of Courland, and after 1559 it lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s former lands, later the District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piltene">Pilten</a>. It then passed to the Duchy of Courland and with it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aizpute.jpg" alt="Aizpute" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgars Šulcs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aizputes_Sv.J%C4%81%C5%86a_luter%C4%81%C5%86u_bazn%C4%ABca_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aizpute lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, in the valley of the Tebra River, 50 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a>. The town was established on the north-eastern edge of the Bandava hillocks in the Western Courland Uplands, on the high banks of the Tebra. The area was inhabited long before the written sources: there are at least 22 known burial mound sites, and the hillfort of Ormkalns lies about 2 km to the south. In the 9th century the Couronians built Beida Castle on the site of the present town, near the Riga to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> trade and military road.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1248 Dietrich von Grüningen, Master of the Livonian Order, ordered the building of Aizpute Castle on the left bank of the Tebra, and that year is counted as the town's founding. After the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> in 1253 it belonged to the Bishopric of Courland. The Couronians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> rebelled in 1258 and destroyed almost the whole army of the Order at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-durbe">Battle of Durbe</a> in 1260, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> could return only in 1295. Bishop Otto granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights on 17 March 1378, and trade along the Tebra made the town notable.</p><p>In 1559 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-denmark">Frederick II of Denmark</a> bought the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> for 30,000 thalers and left it to his brother Duke Magnus of Holstein. Crop failures, famine and plague in the early 17th century reduced the population catastrophically, and in 1660 the Swedes blocked the trading port on the Saka River with stones under the Treaty of Oliva. From 1611 to 1795 Aizpute was capital of the semi-autonomous District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piltene">Pilten</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed Courland in 1795 it became a district centre, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-jews">Lithuanian Jews</a> were allowed to settle. A narrow-gauge railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a> opened in 1899.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Units of the German Army Group North captured the town on 28 June 1941. On 24 July 1941, 39 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and two Latvian women were shot, and on 27 October 1941 German Security Police with Latvian auxiliary police shot the remaining Jews, more than 330 in all, in a forest near Kalvene. Soviet forces entered on 9 May 1945, and repression and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> followed.</p><p>Aizpute became the centre of Aizpute county in 1819 and of Aizpute Municipality in 2009, and since 2021 it has belonged to South Kurzeme Municipality. It is twinned with Schwerzenbach in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and Karlskrona in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Natives include the Baltic German writer Eduard von Keyserling, born at nearby Tāšu-Padure Manor, and the dancer Tatjana Barbakoff.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aizpute” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishop's lands centred on Pilten and <b>Hasenpoth</b>, with the cathedral at Hasenpoth (Aizpute).</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-courland">Bishopric of Courland</a></li><li><blockquote>The district, scattered around Pilten (Piltene) and <b>Hasenpoth</b> in western Latvia, was not merged into the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia but held directly by the Polish crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-pilten">District of Pilten</a></li></ul></section>
